devtools: devtools.selftest ()
devtools: OK = devtools.selftest ()
devtools: [OK, REPORT] = devtools.selftest ()
devtools: ... = devtools.selftest (CMD)
Check that a configured server starts and speaks cleanly.

devtools.selftest () launches the server as a subprocess, drives a
short session through it in each of the two protocol eras, and prints
each finding as its check ends. A server that answers only one era works
with only some hosts, so both are exercised. The check it exists for is the
one that cannot be made from inside: that every byte written to
standard output was a protocol message. A stray printf, an
unsuppressed statement or a line printed by ~/.octaverc corrupts the
stream, and the only symptom a host can show for it is an unexplained failure
to connect. Standard error is read too, for the complementary claim: the
server may write its own diagnostics there, but a diagnostic the
interpreter raised about the server is a defect nothing else here
would see.

OK = devtools.selftest () returns true when every check passed
and prints nothing, which is the form a test uses.

[OK, REPORT] = devtools.selftest () also returns the cell
array of strings that would have been printed, one per check.

... = devtools.selftest (CMD) tests the shell command
CMD rather than the default one. Give it the exact command from your
host configuration to find out whether that configuration is sound; it must
launch a server that reads standard input and writes standard output, and
nothing else. The default launches the interpreter running this function
against the package directory this file lives in, which works from a source
tree as well as from an installed package.

With the default command the evaluating server is checked as well, and so is
the program server, started with devtools.mcpEval ("Sandbox"): that
it starts, reports the sandbox this machine can give it, "active"
where one can be built and otherwise why not, offers octave_call and
not octave_eval, and runs a call in a process of its own.

See also:
devtools.mcp,
devtools.mcpEval


